            "explain": "<p>First label the cells for the positive class 1: <code>TP = cm[1][1] = 45</code> (truly 1, predicted 1), <code>FP = cm[0][1] = 10</code> (truly 0 but flagged), <code>FN = cm[1][0] = 5</code> (truly 1 but missed), <code>TN = 40</code>. Then the arithmetic: precision = TP / (TP + FP) = 45 / (45 + 10) = 45/55 ≈ <b>0.82</b> — of the 55 flagged samples, 45 were real. Recall = TP / (TP + FN) = 45 / (45 + 5) = 45/50 = <b>0.90</b> — of the 50 real positives, 45 were caught. A swaps the two denominators: precision reads down the predicted-positive <i>column</i>, recall across the true-positive <i>row</i>. C is the same pair of formulas applied to class 0 instead: 40/45 ≈ 0.89 and 40/50 = 0.80. D is neither — 0.85 is the <b>accuracy</b>, (40 + 45) / 100, which answers a different question entirely.</p>"
