            "explain": "<p>Layer by layer. <code>LSTM(64, return_sequences=True)</code> emits its 64-dimensional hidden state at <i>every</i> timestep: <code>(None, 20, 64)</code> — the time axis survives. <code>LSTM(32)</code> defaults to <code>return_sequences=False</code>, so it collapses the sequence to the <i>last</i> timestep's hidden state: <code>(None, 32)</code>. <code>Dense(10)</code> remaps only the last axis, 32 to 10: <code>(None, 10)</code> — one logit vector per sequence, which is what sequence classification wants. A forgets that <code>return_sequences=True</code> keeps the time axis — and without it the second LSTM would crash at build time, because it demands a 3-D sequence input; B keeps the time axis through everything, forgetting that the final LSTM collapses it by default (a <code>Dense</code> applied to <code>(None, 20, 32)</code> really would give <code>(None, 20, 10)</code> — one prediction per timestep, the shape you want for sequence <i>labeling</i>, not classification); D mixes the two confusions.</p>"
